{
  "name": "guard_choke_sag",
  "note": "(anim-guard) The knockout (BRIEF \"Holding a guard\": the forearm across the throat, the guard sags and is lowered to the floor under control). One-shot, 5.5 s (frame i of 48 at i * 5.5 / 47 s; play the phase min(t / 5.5, 1) * 47 / 48, never past frame 47, which would wrap to frame 0). 0-0.6 s the grip weakens and the head goes back onto the holder; 0.6-1.2 s one hand drops off, the knees give; 1.2-1.6 s limp: the arms fall, the knees buckle forward over the planted feet, the head rolls onto the holder's shoulder; 1.6-2.6 s lowered by the holder through a deep knee bend to sitting on the floor (the heels slide forward: deliberate), slumped back against the holder; 2.6-3.9 s laid back flat, the heels sliding out, the arms settling to the sides, the head turned to one side; 3.9-5.5 s lying still on his back (the pelvis 10 cm off the floor, the head about 0.9 m behind the root: the holder steps back as she lowers him). Keys are frames (phase = frame / 48). Angles in degrees in the body frame (x right, y up, z forward): pitch about x (+ the top forward), yaw about y (+ turns right), roll about z (+ the right side up); pelvis offsets in cm from rest (Wren 1.68 m). Arms: flex (+ forward, chest frame), abd (+ out), elbow (+ bent), inward (the forearm turned in), wrist { yaw (twist about the forearm), pitch, roll } local to the forearm (anim-rig, RIG.md). Legs (preview and floor checks; the game feet come from the foot planner until a leg channel exists): flex (+ the knee forward), add (+ the foot in), knee (+ bent), ankle (+ the toes up). Keyed by hand from how a real person moves (no game reference); the feet were placed on the floor and the hands on the holder's forearm with a two-bone IK while authoring, then written here as plain angles: edit these numbers directly.",
